<p>You want to climb faster. You look at the math of watts per kilogram, and the easiest variable to change seems to be the denominator. You decide to drop three kilos.</p>

<p>You cut your portions, skip the carbs at dinner, and ride on water. For the first week, the scale moves. Then Tuesday arrives. You have 4 x 10 minutes at threshold. Ten minutes in, your legs are empty. Your heart rate is high, but your power is 20 watts below target. You finish the session in zone 3, completely depleted.</p>

<p>By the end of the month, you are two kilos lighter. But your functional threshold power (FTP) has dropped by 15 watts. Your W/kg ratio hasn't improved. You are just a smaller, slower cyclist.</p>

<p>This is the caloric deficit trap. General weight loss advice treats exercise as a generic mechanism to burn calories. But cycling performance is strictly tied to glycogen availability. If you starve the system, the system protects itself by reducing output. To lean down as an endurance athlete, you do not just eat less. You periodize your fuel.</p>

<h2>The math of body recomposition</h2>

<p>Your goal is not weight loss. Your goal is fat loss while preserving muscle tissue and aerobic capacity.</p>

<p>General diets fail cyclists because they prescribe a static daily deficit. If your maintenance calories are 2500, a standard diet app tells you to eat 2000 calories every day. But your energy expenditure is not static. On a rest day, you burn 2000 calories. On a three-hour ride day, you burn 4000.</p>

<p>If you eat 2000 calories on that ride day, you are in a 2000-calorie deficit. Your body responds to this massive acute energy shortage by breaking down muscle tissue for amino acids, shutting down non-essential recovery processes, and severely blunting your ability to produce high-end power.</p>

<p>The Triaperformance methodology approaches daily nutrition differently. We do not set an arbitrary daily calorie goal. We set a macro framework that adapts to the day's training load.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Protein is fixed.</strong> You need 2 grams per kilogram of body weight, every day, to preserve muscle mass in a deficit.</li>
  <li><strong>Fat is fixed and low.</strong> Keep it between 40 to 80 grams per day. This supports hormone function without consuming excess energy.</li>
  <li><strong>Carbohydrates fluctuate.</strong> Carbs fill the remaining energy needs. On a rest day, this number is low. On an interval day, this number is high.</li>
</ul>

<p>Carbs are king. They are the only fuel source that can sustain threshold and VO2 max efforts. If you restrict them when the work demands them, the work degrades.</p>

<h2>Fuel the work, starve the rest</h2>

<p>Periodized fueling means matching your carbohydrate intake to the specific metabolic demands of the session you are about to do.</p>

<h3>The hard sessions: Fully fueled</h3>

<p>Threshold intervals, VO2 max repeats, and hard group rides require high glycogen availability. You cannot execute these sessions effectively in a depleted state. If you try, you simply accumulate fatigue without delivering the necessary training stimulus to your cardiovascular system.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>Pre-session:</strong> Consume 1 gram of low-fiber carbohydrates per kilogram of body weight in the hour before you start. Avoid fat and protein here unless the session is hours away, as they slow down gastric emptying.</p>
  <p><strong>In-session:</strong> Start at 60 grams of carbs per hour. For cycling, you must train your gut to tolerate higher amounts, pushing toward 100 to 120 grams per hour for long, hard rides. Dissolve this in your bottles.</p>
</div>

<p>By fueling the hard sessions aggressively, you ensure the numerator in your W/kg equation — your power — stays high. The caloric deficit required for fat loss does not happen during this window. It happens later in the day, or on your easy days.</p>

<h3>The easy sessions: Strategic depletion</h3>

<p>Zone 1 and low Zone 2 rides are fueled primarily by fat oxidation. Because the intensity is low, your body does not require immediate, large doses of glucose to sustain the effort.</p>

<p>Fasted training is useful here. Riding for 60 to 90 minutes at an easy aerobic pace before breakfast encourages your body to utilize fat stores. However, fasted training is strictly for easy aerobic work. If the session includes even a single tempo interval, you must fuel it.</p>

<p>Even on easy days, zero calories is not a strict rule. Caffeine and a small amount of carbohydrates can rescue a flat 60-minute Zone 2 day. If taking 30 grams of carbs gets you out the door and keeps the pedals turning efficiently, it is a better outcome than skipping the ride because you felt lethargic.</p>

<h2>Why strength training is non-negotiable</h2>

<p>When you place the body in a caloric deficit, it looks for tissue to break down for energy. If you are only riding a bike, your body has no physiological reason to hold onto upper body muscle, or even excess lower body mass that isn't actively being recruited for high-torque efforts.</p>

<p>Lifting weights provides the mechanical tension necessary to signal the body to retain muscle tissue. If you lose 4 kilos of body weight but 2 kilos of that is muscle, your threshold power will drop, and your real-world performance will suffer.</p>

<p>This is why every cycling weight loss training plan we write includes integrated strength work. The volume of the lifting is modulated so it doesn't leave your legs too heavy to execute your primary bike workouts.</p>

<h2>The training plans</h2>

<p>A structured plan removes the guesswork of balancing cycling volume, strength training, and recovery. All our weight loss plans for cyclists are built around <strong>power</strong> as the primary metric. Heart rate is useful, but it cannot tell you if your actual output is dropping due to under-fueling. A power meter is required to ensure you are holding your watts while dropping your weight.</p>

<p>Here is how the structure scales based on your availability and experience.</p>

<h3>For the time-crunched athlete</h3>

<p>If you have less than six hours a week to train, volume cannot be the driver of your fat loss. The focus shifts to high-quality indoor intervals and targeted strength work that can be done without a gym membership.</p>

