<h2>Troubleshooting the gut</h2>

<p>As you increase your carbohydrate intake on the bike to protect your training quality, you may encounter gastrointestinal distress. Post-ride diarrhea or bloating is common when athletes first try to push past 60 grams of carbs per hour.</p>

<p>This is a mechanical issue, not a sign that carbs are bad for you. It comes down to two variables: product tolerance and concentration.</p>

<p>If you experience GI issues, first check your concentration versus your intake rate. If you mix 90 grams of carbohydrate powder into a 500ml bottle and drink it in 45 minutes without consuming additional water, the solution in your gut is too concentrated. It will draw water from your body into your intestines, causing distress. You must either dilute the mix with more water or slow down your consumption rate to match the hour.</p>

<p>Will it happen during a race or a hard group ride? Yes, if you haven't tested it. That is exactly why we train the gut during the specific, hard sessions in these plans.</p>

<h2>The calorie counter's objection</h2>

<p>The most common pushback to this methodology is based on basic thermodynamics. <em>"A calorie is a calorie. If I burn more fat by riding fasted every day, and I eat fewer calories overall, I will lose weight faster."</em></p>

<p>The first part is true. If you ride fasted, a higher percentage of the calories you burn during that specific hour will come from fat. But fat loss is dictated by your total energy balance over weeks, not the specific fuel used during a 60-minute ride.</p>

<p>If you ride fasted every day, and chronically under-fuel your intervals, your power output drops. A threshold interval done at 220 watts burns significantly less total energy than a threshold interval done at 260 watts. Over a month, the athlete who fuels their hard sessions performs more total work, stimulates more muscle retention, and burns more total energy than the athlete who starves themselves.</p>

<p>You can starve your way to a lighter body weight. But if you want to be a faster cyclist, you have to eat the carbs, hit the watts, and let the deficit happen in the background.</p>
